    {
      "id": "p0178-b010",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "source": {
        "text": "Undyne also tends to laugh when she's confident. In English, this laugh is spelled \"fuhuhu\". In Japanese,fuhuhu is sometimes associated with polite, friendly laughter and cordial giggling, so the translator localizedfuhuhu as kukukuku, which carries a sense of confidence, stubbornness, and/or cunning.",
        "raw": "Undyne also tends to laugh when she's confident. In English, this laugh is\nspelled \"fuhuhu\". In Japanese,fuhuhu is sometimes associated with polite,\nfriendly laughter and cordial giggling, so the translator localizedfuhuhu as\nkukukuku, which carries a sense of confidence, stubbornness, and/or cunning.",
        "status": "corrected",
        "languages": [
          "en"
        ],
        "bbox": [
          0.29864801,
          0.83399286,
          0.90013059,
          0.90136429
        ],
        "mask_bbox": [
          0.29272132,
          0.83327857,
          0.91129816,
          0.90243571
        ],
        "line_ids": [
          39,
          40,
          41,
          42
        ]
      },
      "translation": {
        "text": "Undyne 信心十足时也常会大笑。英语把这笑声写作“fuhuhu”。但在日语中，fuhuhu 有时让人想到礼貌、友好的笑声或亲切的轻笑。于是译者改用了 kukukuku，这种笑声带有自信、倔强或狡黠的意味。",
        "status": "draft",
